{"type":"message","id":"a80ab0fd","parentId":"b91e7422","timestamp":"2026-03-25T15:10:36.130Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Giving medical support advice**\n\nI know the user needs practical advice regarding medical support, and my focus should be on safety first. It's important for me to avoid discussing dosing specifics. I'll highlight the need for an urgent evaluation so that the user understands the seriousness. I'll ensure my response is clear and helpful while sticking to these important points of safety and urgency! Everyone's well-being is a top priority, right?","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_027e91644f7b6c540169c3fae05b248191871c4ab6efa48cf9\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABpw_riGCuOIKwoSh3106_4L2gg_urELSS6WXV_LIkT1AqYPokiN9XEVbWfctCIJHa73bznqorYQgd-OHhLmXXM21ItufdA6hFoVOSQxaaWeCnFRZO--LAY1XUPOuAZecrs2Y9XNFsLpMQSf1-dwrW53y_uEZ8feODf9vzW9mCtCYlBONWDV9IXItiJJmZqy8HmUfAS55fccArZ8CyXwbX5cpe6Gv9ZnH2HAB-tzVCSxu9qJ3lr3-gOMFbbk8eR1v2igHHBWuBOekTaxG_F2jQZyBx-9kVTmgu7hHenNMYIFFCBB7H2tCVJ-7r0veuL7EY_Pa-bPPxR7FHAAVmy2kgBiIApeqapyiZQ73a8hNW9uckHt3Doi2Dh7e9IgGq1606381xSELiD-Wg7G6mowzt9e0xIbVY-xmqSPhYDcdVfSPdnYkR3MYGyoWu9CKBh8iBva_anAmTEJRs-2n61zBkkgQ9ECJZEtCfH4yp-ta4_YzUFT4KMbAxAV06haSJioUVL722UQFtzrxYPjywCNsj_wiVC4nt0LJuW3awVIK5RBUDn59MiCKV6nYMTVBjXhfFKXiuz2Ip0R8Hh-1DpI_eX9z2WYAN9e0yKRm0c00T2Uq1Lx0czmdTEc73XuXP3ejHryT1T2onpBJ0gGIhNNVdTfDKc_sGfmkjrJ80kHBYstmph3FEWGcUN24yN9dhj7Gl2FI0QlF28QYRhs0QbN9379xPTWdAg7cOxtL6jxwPQ28fqEemr0RiI647jiYLtzgz47e2adfAIzd4jM0Zm_q_GQ-4GGrLG9v-FdmG-QFW99Heiep387jaZFNM_1BpIyiBa3EHIDXwvsbTxsX6JRhUGgDR1MiPnFmswYrhBUgC7uvAJtXbKvrjaU9fZ4D4ba5jJM0B7BEdxjh85PxfpijZmBBebcjBlhWcRjIurRglbsfSGT_iP_FqDdC53j_paXDXBUwbaFh18sQxgE2CwFi9SKifV8w==\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Giving medical support advice**\\n\\nI know the user needs practical advice regarding medical support, and my focus should be on safety first. It's important for me to avoid discussing dosing specifics. I'll highlight the need for an urgent evaluation so that the user understands the seriousness. I'll ensure my response is clear and helpful while sticking to these important points of safety and urgency! Everyone's well-being is a top priority, right?\"}]}"},{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] **What to do right now**\nGiven your **severe right-sided pain, pink urine, major recent weight loss, fatigue, nausea, active sunitinib treatment, and prior right kidney cancer history**, I would treat this as something that needs **prompt medical attention**, not something to watch for days at home. If your pain is currently severe, if you feel weak/faint, if urine becomes clearly red, if you are vomiting, or if you are passing very little urine, go to **urgent care / the ER today**.\n\n**How to handle the next few hours**\nTry not to be alone if you are feeling weak or if the pain is escalating suddenly. Keep your **phone charged**, keep **water available** if you can tolerate fluids, and avoid strenuous activity, heavy lifting, or anything that worsens the pain. Because nausea and kidney issues can make dehydration more dangerous, take **small sips of fluid** unless a clinician has told you to restrict fluids.\n\n**What to tell the doctor clearly**\nWhen you contact your oncology/urology team or go in, say this directly:\n“I have a history of right kidney cancer and CKD, I am on sunitinib, and over the last 2 months I’ve had severe worsening right flank/back pain, pink urine, exhaustion, nausea, and 24 kg unintentional weight loss.”\nThat phrasing helps signal that this is **time-sensitive**.\n\n**What to bring with you**\nBring or have ready:\n- your **CT report from last week**\n- your medication list, especially **sunitinib**\n- your most recent lab results if you have them\n- a short symptom timeline: when pain started, when hematuria appeared, how much weight you lost, and whether the pain comes in waves\n\n**How to reduce risk while waiting**\nAvoid **NSAID painkillers** such as ibuprofen, naproxen, or similar unless your own clinician specifically told you they are safe for you, because with **CKD** they can be a poor choice. Avoid alcohol, and do not ignore reduced urine output or worsening nausea.\n\n**What symptoms mean “go now”**\nPlease seek urgent in-person care **immediately** if any of these happen:\n- pain stays at **8-10/10**\n- urine becomes **red or contains clots**\n- you feel **faint, confused, short of breath, or unable to stand well**\n- repeated vomiting or inability to keep fluids down\n- fever/chills\n- you are urinating much less than usual\n\n**Emotional side of this**\nThis is understandably scary, but the best way to regain control is to move from uncertainty into **rapid medical review**. Focus on the next concrete steps: contact your cancer team now, get seen urgently if symptoms are active, and have the recent CT and current symptoms reviewed together.\n\nIf you want, I can help you right now draft a **short urgent message** to send to your oncology/urology team.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_027e91644f7b6c540169c3fae2d28c8191a1c79fa61d7cae88\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":13157,"output":647,"cacheRead":33024,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":46828,"cost":{"input":0.032892500000000005,"output":0.009705,"cacheRead":0.008256,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0508535}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1774451420058}}
